    "source_number": "774",
    "independent_solution": "a) Plot y=sqrt(x) on x>=0 and y=6-x; equality requires additionally 6-x>=0, so 0<=x<=6. The first curve increases and the line decreases: exactly one crossing, (4,2). Algebra independently verifies x=(6-x)^2 -> (x-4)(x-9)=0; x=9 is extraneous because 6-9=-3. b) The common domain is x>0, excluding the hyperbola's pole at zero. Plot increasing y=sqrt(x) against positive decreasing y=6/x; their single crossing is (6^(2/3),6^(1/3)), approximately (3.302,1.817). Let t=sqrt(x)>0: t=6/t^2 -> t^3=6. This exact verification cannot substitute for the requested plotting and reading a noninteger intersection. The written tasks include drawing both graph pairs, using the common visible domain, recognizing uniqueness, and reading x-coordinates; absent actual graphs means graph construction and graph-reading capacity are lost even if the algebra/key is exact."
  },
  "exercise": {
    "number": "774",
    "text": "774. \u7528\u56fe\u89e3\u6cd5\u89e3\u65b9\u7a0b\uff1a\na) $\\sqrt{x}=6-x$\uff1b\nb) $\\sqrt{x}=\\frac{6}{x}$\u3002",
